WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.700
Can one request from the internet
take over our web server?

00:00:04.055 --> 00:00:09.905
On 21 October 2026,
the PHP project fixed two critical flaws.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.750
They need no login.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.350
Three questions matter.

00:00:14.717 --> 00:00:16.107
First, are we updated?

00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:18.482
The flaws are in PHP itself,

00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:19.942
not in our website.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:24.777
One request, with no login,
can run an attacker's code on the server.

00:00:25.097 --> 00:00:26.747
Both are rated critical.

00:00:27.037 --> 00:00:30.427
PHP says its own settings
reduce some attack paths,

00:00:30.677 --> 00:00:33.087
but only the update makes a server safe.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:37.307
Second, is any of our
PHP too old for a fix?

00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:41.492
PHP 8.1 and older get no official release.

00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:45.072
Linux distributions may
ship a patch as a package.

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:49.647
Moving an old application is
rarely a job for a single day.

00:00:49.857 --> 00:00:52.367
So it needs a budget, an owner and a date.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.360
Third, where does PHP run that
we do not manage ourselves?

00:00:57.795 --> 00:00:59.525
Hosted websites, plugins,

00:01:00.195 --> 00:01:01.805
container images and appliances.

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:03.905
There we often wait for the vendor.

00:01:04.295 --> 00:01:08.335
So the question becomes:
which suppliers run PHP for us,

00:01:08.335 --> 00:01:10.465
and have they confirmed the update?

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:12.660
What good looks like.

00:01:13.025 --> 00:01:18.875
NIS2 Article 21 requires vulnerability
handling and supply chain security.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.540
In practice that is a version
list with end-of-support dates,

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.480
an upgrade plan with a date, and written
confirmation from every supplier.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.730
A software bill of materials
in contracts answers

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.310
"where do we run PHP" from a list.

00:01:35.197 --> 00:01:36.407
A suggested first step.

00:01:36.692 --> 00:01:39.622
Ask which internet-facing systems run PHP,

00:01:40.172 --> 00:01:42.862
including those that suppliers run for us.

00:01:43.097 --> 00:01:45.307
Which run a version with no official fix?

00:01:45.692 --> 00:01:50.662
And who confirms in writing that each
is updated or isolated, and by when?

00:01:50.942 --> 00:01:53.252
Update now, then check for intruders.

00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:56.467
Two flaws, three questions.

00:01:56.842 --> 00:01:59.792
Update first, then list every PHP we run.

